CORE smt-backend
main.c
--z3 --static-coverage
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
^Static coverage warnings:$
^.*\[unconstrained code .*\] main.c:.*$
^.*\[unnecessary assumption\] main.c:.*$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR
^line \d+ column \d+: unknown constant
--
End-to-end regression test for the Tomb & Joshi static-coverage
warning emission path. Exercises:

  * the SMT2 unsat-core fix in smt2_conv.cpp (literals separated
    by spaces in the check-sat-assuming list — without it Z3
    fails to parse `truetruetrueB0...`),
  * the --static-coverage CLI flag implying --proof-explanation,
  * the report_util output_static_coverage_warnings() emitter,
  * classify_uncovered() correctly mapping not-in-core
    assumptions to UNNECESSARY_ASSUMPTION and not-in-core
    assignments to UNCONSTRAINED_CODE.

The negative-side regex on the SMT2 parser error guards against
the bug we found and fixed: `line ... column ... unknown constant`
showed up when literals were concatenated.

The branchy main intentionally produces non-constant guards on
the if-branches so the unsat-core mechanism has labeled
assumptions to discriminate. Top-level straight-line code
produces constant-true guards which the conflict_provider
machinery skips (this is by design — see proof_explanation.cpp
around line 343).
